# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# authprogs rules for the aws1 -> trixie migration.
#
# Append to /root/.ssh/authprogs.conf on aws1, then remove both lines once the
# migration is done.
#
# Format is Bri Hatch's authprogs: a bracketed list of source IPs, then one
# allowed command per line, matched EXACTLY - no regex, no shell
# metacharacters. That constraint shapes everything below: each entry is a
# no-argument wrapper script, because a command with arguments cannot be
# matched loosely and a command with a fixed argument list cannot be reused.
#
# Both IPs are listed because the Elastic IP moves during the cutover: the new
# box is 16.147.33.237 now and becomes 34.212.49.11 afterwards. Without the
# second, a rule that worked all through the rehearsal would start being denied
# at the one moment it matters.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[ 16.147.33.237 34.212.49.11 ]
    /root/mig/dump-for-migration
    /root/mig/send-migration-tar

# Install the two wrappers on aws1 first:
#
#   cd /home/cambell/imscp/migrate/scripts
#   sudo install -m 0700 -o root -g root aws1-dump-for-migration  /root/mig/dump-for-migration
#   sudo install -m 0700 -o root -g root aws1-send-migration-tar  /root/mig/send-migration-tar
#
# Deliberately NOT added:
#
#   mysql       `mysql -e '<sql>'` is arbitrary SQL as root - every customer
#               database readable and writable. dump-for-migration runs the
#               only queries the migration needs.
#   mysqldump   free arguments include --result-file, which writes anywhere as
#               root.
#   rsync       cannot be pinned safely here. The remote command it asks for
#               encodes the client's flags and version, so an exact-match entry
#               breaks on the next rsync upgrade. send-migration-tar replaces
#               it with one stable spelling.
